<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-ch-01-key-concepts" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Key Concepts</title>

  <p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Water is polar, meaning it has a partially positive side and a
          partially negative side.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Salts like sodium chloride ( <m>\ce{NaCl}</m> ) are ionic, meaning the
          atoms form full positive and negative charges.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Oils are nonpolar, meaning they do not have partially positive or
          negative parts.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Polar substances, like water, only dissolve other polar substances.
          Oil does not dissolve in water, so it is called hydrophobic.
          Salt dissolves in water, so it is called hydrophilic.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Soap molecules are amphiphilic, meaning they have both hydrophilic and
          hydrophobic/lipophilic parts.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          When soap is dissolved in water, the molecules form spherical clusters
          called micelles that trap hydrophobic residues.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Soaps act as surfactants by disturbing the surface tension of water,
          allowing it to reach tiny crevices in the skin.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Soaps kill viruses with lipid bilayers by adsorbing onto the bilayer
          and destroying it completely.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          When exposed to hard water, traditional soaps form soap scum by
          binding to charged minerals.
          Modern synthetic detergents use different hydrophilic heads to resolve
          this.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          pH is a measure of acidity, with lower pH being more acidic.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Traditional bar soaps are highly alkaline and may interfere with the
          acid mantle of the skin.
          Modern syndets can be formulated at skin-neutral pH to resolve this.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Temperature is not a significant factor in handwashing.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Antibacterial hand soaps do not offer significantly better bacteria
          removal than traditional soaps, and come with risks like contributing
          to antibiotic resistance.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Hot-air dryers do not work as well as paper towels due to lack of
          friction, toilet particles in the air, and damp skin.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
</section>
